These lemmas are **jointly 1-inductive after a sampled reset**. The target’s overlapped implication checks `sn` in the same cycle as `~in`. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/resources/videos/SystemVerilog_Assertions_Tutorial_2016.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (i <= 8'd9); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset sets i to 1; the loop increments it only through 9. This bound excludes unreachable counter values needed for the phase invariant."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (sn == (i - 8'd1)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset establishes the relation; i and sn then increment together or both hold. It maps i=1 to sn=0 and i=9 to sn=8."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (!in |-> (i == 8'd1 || i == 8'd9)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With lemma_1, an inactive loop flag occurs only before the first increment or after completion. Combined with lemma_2, these two cases prove prop."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

Because reset is synchronous, the proof must assume `rst` is sampled high at least once; `disable iff (rst)` alone does not constrain arbitrary pre-reset state.

`|->` is an **overlapping** implication, so `prop` checks `sn` on the same clock edge where `in` is low. The following invariants connect that condition to the counter state. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (i >= 8'd1 && i <= 8'd9); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset sets i to 1; the loop increments it only through 9, where it stops. This bounds the states considered by the remaining lemmas."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (i == (sn + 8'd1)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Reset establishes the relation, and every increment advances i and sn together. It maps i=1 to sn=0 and i=9 to sn=8."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (!in |-> (i == 8'd1 || i == 8'd9)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The bounded machine has in low only at the reset-start state or after the loop finishes. Combined with lemma_2, these two states give exactly the sn values required by prop."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The proof assumes reset is asserted to establish the initial state; the registers otherwise have no specified initial values.
